# Vendored verify core — the OFFLINE receipt verifier (1307) and the CANONICAL EVIDENCE core (1423).
#
# Byte copies from the public receipt-verifier, pinned. The guard used to verify over the
# NETWORK via config.client.verifyReceipt(), which the other three enforcement points do not:
# contract-gate, k8s-admission and gateway-verifier all verify offline against a pinned keyring.
# A verifier that needs the network fails differently from one that does not, and the sentence
# "the same proof at all four points, offline" was false because of this one.
#
# 1431 ADDS THE GRANT. Measured before it was changed: the guard authenticated the receipt (this
# verify.js) and the executor attestation (SDK verifyExecutionAttestation), and NEVER the
# execution grant. `parseGrantFields` in the SDK DECODES a grant to cross-check jti/scope_hash
# against the attestation, so a forged token whose payload copies those fields — with the word
# NEM-ALAIRAS where the signature belongs — was accepted as a kernel binding and upgraded
# ENFORCING_STRICT from authorized_not_committed to authorized_and_committed. A binding checked
# against an unauthenticated document is not a binding.
#
# Do not edit these files here. Recopy from receipt-verifier and re-pin.
# PER-FILE PROVENANCE, RETIRED. It said verify.js was held at 6048195 because "recopying HEAD
# would change a verifier nobody measured again" — true when written. MEASURED before retiring it:
# the 6048195 -> v1.0.0 delta on verify.js is ZERO non-comment lines (CLI usage docs), so the
# reason did not apply to this delta. verify-evidence.js was older still and did not export
# verifyEvidenceRootBinding at all, which is why the shared core needed a fail-closed guard against
# its own missing dependency. The core is now ONE released tag.
#
#   verify.js                   6048195c0bcf7e2594a2d78e83cfc082ea615790
#   arity.js                    724aa9ae8171f1acf629c3c6b4879dc056575f20
#   verify-grant.js             a472bf9073781cba25d83c7d1406b1a7c4af0b94
#   verify-prove-transcript.js  724aa9ae8171f1acf629c3c6b4879dc056575f20
#   verify-evidence.js          724aa9ae8171f1acf629c3c6b4879dc056575f20
#   keys/coderifts-keys.json    724aa9ae8171f1acf629c3c6b4879dc056575f20

# ── THE RECEIPT-VERIFIER CORE IS PINNED TO A RELEASED TAG ───────────────────────────────────
#
# The shared verification core here comes from receipt-verifier **v1.0.3**, peeled commit
# 523e0a2b822088990f500b9eea3868309439c53c.
#
# It was pinned to a WORKING TREE and to per-file commits. Both are honest about where the bytes
# came from and neither is a provenance a stranger can resolve: a working tree is not fetchable,
# so "the vendored bytes match upstream" reduced to "they match whatever is on this machine right
# now". A released tag is a name anyone can check out — that is the difference between a pin and
# a note.
#
# Hash lines that moved: verify.js, verify-grant.js (v1.0.3 admits applied_policy_hash).
# Every other hash line is unchanged because those files are byte-identical to v1.0.2.
#
#
# ── AND THE TAG IS SIGNED, WHICH IS THE PART A STRANGER CAN CHECK ───────────────────────────
#
# v1.0.0 was an unsigned tag: a name anyone can check out, and also a name anyone with push access
# can move. v1.0.1 is annotated and SSH-signed, so the pin now resolves to an identity and not
# only to bytes.
#
#   tag object   bb5753681275920e7e23351c6b41632957e6238f
#   peeled commit 523e0a2b822088990f500b9eea3868309439c53c
#   signer       zsobpeter@gmail.com
#   fingerprint  SHA256:7yRXTm9zKGicfFpzL+7lpwFoPaoSwxAJlabB3jwxw2Y
#
# Verify it yourself, in a checkout of receipt-verifier:
#
#   git tag -v v1.0.3
#   -> Good "git" signature for zsobpeter@gmail.com with ED25519 key SHA256:7yRXTm9zKGicfFpzL+7lpwFoPaoSwxAJlabB3jwxw2Y
#
# WHY v1.0.3 AND NOT v1.0.2: the CORE IS NOT byte-identical. verify-grant.js admits
# applied_policy_hash on V2_RESERVED_INERT (1942 verifier-admits; fail-closed otherwise
# unchanged) and verify.js moved with it. This re-pin copies those bytes from the signed
# tag, not from a working tree.
#
# The peeled commit is 523e0a2 — the K-phase-1 inert-admit commit the tag names.
# source_commit: 523e0a2b822088990f500b9eea3868309439c53c (receipt-verifier v1.0.3, signed)
#

verify.js  52d7c37611f9eecc862e3678d1ffcb4fd860ea4c5faf9a9df6d6d5b290bd8197
